fix(session): derive cookie Secure flag from request scheme
---
## Problem

`SESSION_SECURE_COOKIE` defaulted to `true` (#1003), so any server serving plaintext http (e.g. `php artisan serve` on `:8000`) hit the CSRF 419 "Page Expired" wall at startup: browsers refuse to store a `Secure` cookie over http, so the session/CSRF cookie never came back and login was unfixable from the UI.

Produced on a fresh server hitting `http://<host>:8000/login`.

## Fix

`config/session.php` no longer forces the flag. Unset/empty env now delegates to Symfony, which marks the cookie `Secure` only when the request itself is HTTPS:

- direct fastcgi/nginx HTTPS: `Secure` automatically
- `X-Forwarded-Proto: https` honored only through proxies pinned in `TRUSTED_PROXIES` (#855 fail-closed posture preserved — unpinned XFP never upgrades the flag)
- explicit `true`/`false` still force (e2e `:8001` carve-out untouched)

## Templates

`.env.example`, `.env.example.mysql`, `.env.example.pgsql`, `.env-example-github` now ship the line commented out, so freshly provisioned servers boot into the derived behavior instead of re-bricking plaintext logins.

## Tests

`tests/Feature/SessionCookieSecureTest.php` rewritten — 8 tests pin every branch:

- derived flag: plain http → non-Secure (the regression), full https → `Secure`
- proxy: pinned `TRUSTED_PROXIES` + XFP https → `Secure`; unpinned XFP → ignored (#855)
- overrides: `true`, `false`, and `""` behaving exactly like unset (`env()` returns `""` not `null` for empty `.env` values — a bare string would silently coerce to `false` in `Symfony\Component\HttpFoundation\Cookie`)
- `http_only` / `same_site` defaults preserved

Previously: the same test file pinned the forced-`true` default.

